        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "KingSmith WalkingPad", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/kingsmith-walking-pad/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is WalkingPad a brand or a type of product?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both, which is the source of endless confusion. WalkingPad is KingSmith's product line — the company built the first mass-market slim under-furniture treadmill and named it. The word then escaped into general use as a category term, the way hoover and biro did. So a search for \"walking pad\" returns the whole category, not this brand.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Who makes WalkingPad treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "KingSmith, a Chinese manufacturer that developed the format in the second half of the 2010s. Unlike much of this category — which is dominated by marketplace storefronts that contracted a factory to copy the category leader — KingSmith is an actual product company with a design history in this specific format, which is the main practical argument for the brand.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are KingSmith walking pads worth the extra money?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends how long you intend to keep it. If you treat a pad as semi-disposable and expect to replace it in three years, much of the premium is irrelevant and a cheaper machine often matches it on specification. If you want it still working in five years, the parts channel and company continuity are real advantages — a controller board only exists if someone is still making one.",
